abstract: "Replacing a classifier with a smaller version can reduce computation, but verifying that the replacement preserves accuracy may require expensive labels. We present a shared finite-pool audit that queries only inputs on which at least one candidate disagrees with a reference model. Exact hypergeometric count bounds control the excess classification error simultaneously across candidates and predetermined inspection budgets. One queried label can inform several comparisons, and a completed disagreement census gives an exact answer. We evaluate 240 replacements from random-forest and extremely randomized-tree ensembles on six public datasets, using five training splits and 500 audit repetitions per model family. At a 256-label budget and a one-percentage-point error tolerance, the audit certifies a replacement in 83.4% of runs, versus 49.8% for uniform conditional auditing, while reducing the selected ensemble’s tree count by 64.3% on average. Four of 30,000 audit trajectories contain an erroneous certification across all inspection points. In 34 controlled stress scenarios, maximum simultaneous undercoverage is 1.71% at a 5% allowance. The results identify when prediction agreement makes model replacement inexpensive to verify and when uncertainty requires retaining the reference. The guarantee concerns the fixed evaluation pool, not future-distribution accuracy."
keywords:
  - "active testing"
  - "finite population inference"
  - "model replacement"
  - "risk certification"
